            "The single most useful thing a reader can take from this chapter is the difference between a stock and a flow. Emissions are a flow, measured in tonnes per year, and they can be cut with policy in a decade. Concentrations are a stock, measured in parts per million, and they respond to the cumulative total rather than to the current rate. Temperature responds to the stock, sea level responds to the temperature with a delay of decades to centuries, and ice sheets respond to the sea level with a delay of centuries more. Almost every political argument about climate policy is really an argument about which of those four clocks the speaker is looking at, and most of the disagreement dissolves once the clocks are named. A government that has cut its annual emissions by half has done something real and has not yet changed the stock by very much; a government that has promised to reach net zero by 2050 has made a commitment about a flow in a year when it will not be in office.",
